WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.320
Before we get deep into the details of building a&nbsp;
database and then querying parts of a database, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.360
need to understand what we are looking at within a&nbsp;
database. I have an instance of SQL Server up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
running with the sample AdventureWorks database,&nbsp;
which will be used heavily in this course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.120
displayed in the Object Explorer. With&nbsp;
a database, data is stored in tables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.360
otherwise known as entities, and this database&nbsp;
has roughly 70 tables used to store information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.480
To see what is in one of these entities, I&nbsp;
will select and hold (or right-click) a table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:40.240
specifically this Person.Person table, and I will&nbsp;
choose Select Top 100 Rows. This option is good to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.360
use when you are trying to get familiar with a&nbsp;
database and the tables inside of it. We see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.400
structure of the entity plus some data within the&nbsp;
entity. A line within an entity is known as a row&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:59.280
or a record. Entities store data using records.&nbsp;
We see a name and a little bit of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.480
about the person within the record for each entry.&nbsp;
Whereas records are rows of information, fields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:11.600
seen here, are columns of information. Just as&nbsp;
the terms entity and table are interchangeable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.840
and the terms row and record are interchangeable,&nbsp;
the terms column and field are interchangeable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.800
Columns are entity properties that define the data&nbsp;
to be stored in records. As we will learn later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:32.160
we can also define the type of data to store in&nbsp;
each column. For example, the name fields store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:41.360
text while this email promotion field is probably&nbsp;
a number and the ModifiedDate field is a date and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:48.640
time field. Recap: An entity stores information, a&nbsp;
row or record is a line of data within an entity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.583
and a column or field is a property that helps&nbsp;
define the data stored within an entity's records.

